Hardening patch proposal: lib/freebl/hmacct.c::MAC() -- enforce the invariant
overhang computation depends on, instead of borrowing it from the one current caller.

Context (full writeup in AUDIT_hmacct_MAC.md): `overhang = headerLen - mdBlockSize`
underflows as an unsigned int if `headerLen < mdBlockSize`. The one real caller
(ssl3con.c's internal SSLv3/TLS record-MAC code) always supplies headerLen=71 or 75
(fixed by protocol format), so this never fires today -- but nothing in hmacct.c or
softoken's SetupMAC()/sftk_SSLv3MACConstantTime_New() enforces the invariant locally;
it is borrowed entirely from that one caller's behavior. A future or alternate
PKCS#11-layer caller supplying a short/empty MAC key to CKM_SSL3_MD5_MAC/SHA1_MAC
would violate it: headerLen could drop below mdBlockSize (64), producing a
multi-gigabyte out-of-bounds read (from a 75-byte header buffer) and write (into a
144-byte firstBlock) via the underflowed `overhang`.

Fix: check the invariant explicitly, at the point that depends on it, rather than
trusting the caller. MAC() currently has no failure return path at all (always
returns SECSuccess) -- this patch adds one, consistent with how its own callers
(HMAC_ConstantTime / SSLv3_MAC_ConstantTime) already propagate a SECStatus and are
already called through PORT_CheckSuccess by softoken.

--- a/lib/freebl/hmacct.c
+++ b/lib/freebl/hmacct.c
@@ -174,6 +174,13 @@ static SECStatus
     if (k > 0) {
         if (isSSLv3) {
+            /* The overhang computation below assumes headerLen >= mdBlockSize (true
+             * for every real SSLv3/TLS header this function is documented to accept
+             * -- see the top-of-file comment -- but not otherwise enforced anywhere
+             * in this call chain). Check it explicitly rather than borrow the
+             * invariant from the one caller that currently happens to satisfy it:
+             * an unsigned underflow here would turn into a multi-gigabyte
+             * out-of-bounds read of |header| and write into |firstBlock|. */
+            if (headerLen < mdBlockSize) {
+                PORT_SetError(SEC_ERROR_INPUT_LEN);
+                return SECFailure;
+            }
             /* The SSLv3 header is larger than a single block.
              * overhang is the number of bytes beyond a single
              * block that the header consumes: either 7 bytes

Also apply the same style of check to write 1 (line ~152), upgrading the
already-dead-in-release PORT_Assert to a real check -- not because it is reachable
today (SetupMAC()'s own `secretLength > sizeof(secret)` check already bounds it to
<=64, comfortably under hmacPad's 144-byte capacity), but because MAC()'s own local
bound (`sizeof(hmacPad)` = 144) is looser than what SetupMAC() enforces (<=64) --
a future caller supplying a secret between 65 and 144 bytes would be safe for MAC()
itself but currently rejected one frame up by a stricter, unrelated check. Making
MAC() assert its own real bound decouples the two and documents the actual local
contract:

--- a/lib/freebl/hmacct.c
+++ b/lib/freebl/hmacct.c
@@ -149,7 +149,10 @@ static SECStatus
         bits += 8 * mdBlockSize;
         memset(hmacPad, 0, mdBlockSize);
-        PORT_Assert(macSecretLen <= sizeof(hmacPad));
+        if (macSecretLen > sizeof(hmacPad)) {
+            PORT_SetError(SEC_ERROR_INPUT_LEN);
+            return SECFailure;
+        }
         memcpy(hmacPad, macSecret, macSecretLen);

Validation this patch would need before landing (not done here -- this is a proposal,
not an applied/tested change):
  - NSS's own test suite (softoken PKCS#11 MAC mechanism tests, SSL/TLS record-MAC
    tests) -- confirm the new failure paths are never hit by real traffic, and that
    HMAC_ConstantTime/SSLv3_MAC_ConstantTime's existing SECFailure handling in
    sftk_*ConstantTime_Update (via PORT_CheckSuccess) degrades sanely rather than
    crashing when MAC() newly returns failure.
  - Constant-time review: the new `if` at the top of the isSSLv3 branch is a
    length-dependent (not secret-dependent) branch -- headerLen/mdBlockSize are
    public protocol parameters, not the secret data this function exists to process
    in constant time, so this should not reopen a timing side-channel, but that
    reasoning should be checked against NSS's own constant-time review process
    before landing, not assumed here.

Not filed upstream -- this is a local proposal for review, per the exploratory-scan
scope (no CVE-based selection, no changes committed to any frozen branch).
